From 9d7186f8844ad4f068382d2bcdf7fcc1e8894577 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: JingMatrix <jingmatrix@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 01:29:50 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 29/47] Fabricate a hardware attestation identity when the
 device has none
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A software-only device (an emulator with no TEE) or one whose TEE is broken
captures only a Software-level attestation, which apps reject as not hardware-backed.
Present a fabricated TrustedEnvironment identity in that case: the harvest keeps the
real captured values but records a fabricated securityLevel and the OS-appropriate
attestation/keymaster versions, and the resolver emits those to the TA. A device
that really attested in hardware passes its captured values through unchanged.

The reference TA tied attestationVersion to keymintVersion through the KeyMint HAL
enum (100/200/300/400), so it could not emit the Keymaster versions a pre-KeyMint
device reports. It now carries a raw attestationVersion and derives keymintVersion
from it (Keymaster 3.0 = attestation 2 / keymaster 3, 4.0 = 3 / 4, 4.1 = 4 / 41;
KeyMint N.0 = N*100 for both), matching what a real device of each OS release reports.

The harvest panel presents both sides: the two hardware levels as chips
(TrustedEnvironment by default, and StrongBox; never Software), the values actually
captured — struck through in red where replaced — and, under Fabricated, the chip's
security level and the versions handed to apps.
